            make_q("What is an 'oblivious decision tree' (symmetric tree) as used by CatBoost?", ["A tree that forgets its weights", "A tree where the exact same splitting criterion is applied across all nodes at the same depth level, creating balanced, highly cache-efficient trees", "A tree with no root", "A tree that ignores the target variable"], 1, "Oblivious trees use identical split rules across each depth level, allowing vector-level CPU SIMD evaluation and preventing overfitting."),
            make_q("What does the `subsample` (stochastic gradient boosting) parameter control in GBMs?", ["The fraction of features dropped", "The fraction of training samples randomly drawn without replacement to train each individual sequential tree (typically 0.7 - 0.8)", "The batch size in epochs", "The number of output classes"], 1, "Subsampling rows (e.g. `subsample=0.8`) introduces stochastic regularization and speeds up training iterations."),
            make_q("What does `colsample_bytree` control in XGBoost / LightGBM?", ["The number of trees in the forest", "The fraction of features randomly subsampled when constructing each individual tree", "The number of rows in the CSV", "The column names"], 1, "Feature subsampling (`colsample_bytree`) prevents trees from over-relying on a few dominant predictors."),
            make_q("What is Early Stopping in Gradient Boosting?", ["Halting training when validation loss fails to improve for a specified number of consecutive iterations (`n_iter_no_change` / `early_stopping_rounds`)", "Stopping the server manually", "A Python exception", "A hardware watchdog timer"], 0, "Early stopping monitors held-out validation loss, automatically freezing the model at its peak generalization iteration."),
            make_q("What is the typical depth of base decision trees used in Gradient Boosting compared to Random Forests?", ["GBM uses deep trees (depth 20); Random Forest uses stumps (depth 1)", "GBM uses shallow trees (depth 3 to 6, weak learners); Random Forest typically uses deep, unconstrained trees (depth 15+)", "Both use identical depth 50", "GBM does not use trees"], 1, "Boosting requires weak learners with high bias (shallow trees, depth 3-6); Bagging requires low-bias, high-variance learners (deep trees)."),
            make_q("Why is a base learner in Gradient Boosting called a 'weak learner'?", ["Because it has code bugs", "It is an estimator that performs only slightly better than random guessing (e.g. shallow decision tree with restricted depth)", "It runs very slowly", "It has negative accuracy"], 1, "Weak learners have restricted capacity (high bias, low variance); boosting iteratively combines them to build a strong learner."),
            make_q("What is the `scale_pos_weight` parameter in XGBoost / LightGBM used for?", ["Scaling image pixel values", "Balancing positive and negative class penalties in imbalanced classification (typically set to $\\frac{\\text{negative count}}{\\text{positive count}}$)", "Multiplying predictions by 10", "A hyperparameter for regression only"], 1, "`scale_pos_weight` scales the gradient of the positive class, compensating for severe class imbalance without resampling."),
